    "result_text": "No stale references remain. The change is complete and the test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test run, 0 failures).\n\n## What I changed\n\n**The tier \u2192 level rule.** `membershipLevel` is now computed at the response edge as: start at 1; +1 if an email is present; +1 if `namesakeCount` is 0; capped at 3 (level 4 left for tenure). The old string tier's `householdSize`-based GOLD logic is gone.\n\n- **`model/MembershipLevel.java`** (new) \u2014 a small derivation helper mirroring the existing `Locality.of(...)` pattern, holding the level formula in one place.\n- **`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 replaced the `membershipTier` MapStruct expression (which produced `MembershipTierEnum.GOLD/SILVER/BRONZE`) with `membershipLevel` = `MembershipLevel.of(owner)`.\n- **`resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 replaced the `membershipTier` string/enum property with `membershipLevel` (integer, min 1, max 3), so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes the numeric field and drops the enum.\n- **`rest/function/owner/AuditOwnerCreated.java`** \u2014 the create audit line now records `membershipLevel={}` (the thing the spec says previously recorded the tier).\n- **`rest/function/owner/AssignHouseholdSize.java`** \u2014 removed the now-false \"`membershipTier` is GOLD when it is 3 or more\" doc comment.\n\nThe acceptance test (`membershipLevel` == 3 for a uniquely-named owner with an email) passes, and the audit output confirms `membershipLevel=3`.\n\nOne thing worth flagging: the spec's parenthetical implied the create audit line already *recorded the tier*, but in this codebase it recorded `id/customerCode/registrationDate` and never the tier. I read the spec's intent as \"the audit should carry membership standing, now as a level\" and added `membershipLevel` to it rather than inventing a tier field to replace. No test asserts the audit format, so this is safe either way \u2014 but if you'd prefer the audit line left untouched, that's a one-line revert.",
